    "id": 99,
    "topic": "BigQuery",
    "difficulty": 2,
    "question": "You have a query that filters a BigQuery table using a WHERE clause on timestamp and ID columns. By using bq query --dry_run you learn that the query triggers a full scan of the table, even though the filter on timestamp and ID select a tiny fraction of the overall data. You want to reduce the amount of data scanned by BigQuery with minimal changes to existing SQL queries. What should you do?",
    "options": [
      "A. Create a separate table for each ID.",
      "B. Use the LIMIT keyword to reduce the number of rows returned.",
      "C. Recreate the table with a partitioning column and clustering column.",
      "D. Use the bq query --maximum_bytes_billed flag to restrict the number of bytes billed."
    ],
    "correct": 2,
    "explanation": "Partition on the timestamp and cluster on ID so the existing WHERE filters prune data, cutting bytes scanned with no change to the SQL.",
    "discussion": [
      {
        "user": "rickywck",
        "text": "should be C:\n\nhttps://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/best-practices-costs"
      },
      {
        "user": "MaxNRG",
        "text": "https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/best-practices-costs\n\nApplying a LIMIT clause to a SELECT * query does not affect the amount of data read. You are billed for reading all bytes in the entire table, and the query counts against your free tier quota.\n\nA and D doesnt make sense\n\nIts C, when you want to select by a partition you should write something like:\n\nCREATE TABLE `blablabla.partitioned`\n\nPARTITION BY\n\nDATE(timestamp)\n\nCLUSTER BY id\n\nAS\n\nSELECT * FROM `blablabla`"
      },
      {
        "user": "Crudgey",
        "text": "Are they having a laugh at us by giving so many bad answers?"
      },
      {
        "user": "sumanshu",
        "text": "Vote for C"
      },
      {
        "user": "felixwtf",
        "text": "LIMIT keyword is applied only at the end, i.e., only to limit the results already calculated. Therefore, a full table scan will have already happened. The where clause on the other hand would provide the desired filtering depending on the case. So, C is the correct answer."
      },
      {
        "user": "PRC",
        "text": "C is correct...Partitioning and Clustering are the best practices to limit the data scanning"
      }
    ],
